/*
==============================================================================
  PoC 01 -- `last()` defeats the safety-formula classifier => unsound induction
==============================================================================

  CLASS      : missed attack (false "verified")
  SEVERITY   : critical -- unsound proof rule
  AFFECTS    : tamarin-prover 1.12.0 and develop @ ef3f0468 (1.13.0)
  STATUS     : confirmed by the maintainers; fix proposed in PR #915 (open).
               The maintainers dispute the "soundness" label.  See the
               DISPUTE section below and README.md.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ROOT CAUSE
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  lib/theory/src/Theory/Constraint/System.hs:849-856

      (safetyRestrictions, otherRestrictions) = partition isSafetyFormula restrictions
      -- Non-safety restrictions must be added to the formula, as they render
      -- the set of traces non-prefix-closed, which makes the use of induction
      -- unsound.
      gf2 = gconj $ gf1 : otherRestrictions

  The classifier this depends on is
  lib/theory/src/Theory/Constraint/System/Guarded.hs:156-164

      isSafetyFormula gf0 = null (frees [gf0]) && noExistential gf0
        where
          noExistential (GAto _)            = True     -- <-- accepts Last atoms
          noExistential (GGuarded Ex _ _ _) = False
          ...

  "Closed and existential-free" is not the same as "prefix-closed".  The atom
  `last(#i)` contains no existential quantifier, so a restriction built from
  `not(last(#i))` is misclassified as a safety restriction.  It is then routed
  into `sLemmas` instead of being conjoined into the proof obligation before
  `ginduct` runs, and the induction hypothesis is asserted over trace prefixes
  that are not themselves valid traces of the restricted system.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHY THE RESTRICTION BELOW IS NOT A SAFETY FORMULA
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Guarded.hs defines a safety formula as one where "a trace violating it can
  never be extended to a trace satisfying it".  Take:

      t1 = [Start(x)]              -- Start IS the last event  => VIOLATES
      t2 = [Start(x), Step(x)]     -- Start is not last        => SATISFIES

  t1 is a prefix of t2, so a violating trace extends to a satisfying one.
  Not a safety formula.  Tamarin nevertheless prints "// safety formula"
  beside it in the output.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  GROUND TRUTH
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Both lemmas below are the SAME formula and both are FALSE: the trace
  [Start(x), Step(x)] satisfies the restriction and contains a Start event.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  TAMARIN SAYS (1.12.0, unpatched)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    NoStart_with_induction    (all-traces): verified (3 steps)     <-- WRONG
    NoStart_without_induction (all-traces): falsified - found trace (2 steps)
    StartReachable            (exists-trace): verified (2 steps)
    /* All wellformedness checks were successful. */

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CONTROL EXPERIMENT (isolates the mechanism)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  01b_control_existential_phrasing.spthy is the SAME protocol and the SAME
  lemma, with the restriction expressed as a guarded existential:

      restriction StartImpliesStep:
        "All x #i. Start(x) @ i ==> Ex #j. Step(x) @ j"

  This is also non-prefix-closed -- [Start(x)] violates it, [Start(x),Step(x)]
  satisfies it -- but the existential makes isSafetyFormula return False, so
  Tamarin conjoins it into the proof obligation instead.  Result:

      last phrasing         : NoStart_with_induction -> verified   (WRONG)
      existential phrasing  : NoStart_with_induction -> falsified  (correct)

  Same protocol, same lemma, both restrictions non-prefix-closed.  The only
  difference is which side of `partition isSafetyFormula` they land on.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DISPUTE: is this a soundness bug?
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The maintainers confirm the bug and dispute the label.  Their position is
  that `last` is intended for internal use only, that not enough places
  checked for it, and that "it's debatable whether this is a soundness bug as
  no proof rule was changed".

  This file takes the position that it is a soundness bug:

    1. PR #915 makes three changes: a warning, a filter that drops such
       restrictions, and a one-line change to `isSafetyFormula`.  Keep only
       the warning and revert the rest, and this file still prints `verified`
       for a false lemma.  The change that corrects the verdict is the change
       to the classifier.

    2. `isSafetyFormula` exists to discharge a side condition that induction
       needs in order to be sound, and it returned the wrong answer for the
       restriction below.

    3. "Internal use only" was not a rule a user could have followed.  The
       manual's machine-tested grammar (manual/grammar/grammar.ebnf) derives
       `last()` from the body of a restriction:

           restriction ::= ... ':' '"' (_formula: formula) '"'
           _formula    ::= ... | _temporal_variable_operation | ...
           _temporal_variable_operation ::= temp_var_induction | temp_var_order
                                          | temp_var_eq
           temp_var_induction ::= ATOM('last' '(' temporal_var ')')

       `last` sits in the same production as `#i < #j` and `#i = #j`, which
       restrictions use constantly.

    4. The hole is as old as the fix.  See PROVENANCE.

  Full argument: README.md, section "On the soundness classification".

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PROVENANCE
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This is a re-entry of soundness bug #108 (Simon Meier, 2012).  Commit
  a32cc3f8 added the original counterexample
  (examples/loops/Axioms_and_Induction.spthy, which uses a non-safety
  restriction with an EXISTENTIAL); commit 9726e4c4 "fixed two soundness bugs"
  introduced `isSafetyFormula` as the fix.

  Verified directly:

    * The body of `isSafetyFormula` at 9726e4c4 (src/Theory/Constraint/System/
      Guarded.hs) and at HEAD (lib/theory/src/.../Guarded.hs) are BYTE
      IDENTICAL.  The predicate was never modified.

    * `last` was already surface syntax in that same commit: 9726e4c4's
      src/Theory/Text/Parser.hs:340 contains
          [ Last <$> try (symbol "last" *> parens nodevarTerm) <?> "last atom"

  So the fix and the hole shipped together in August 2012, and the hole was
  open for the entire lifetime of the fix.

  SCOPE: no shipped case study is affected.  All 1042 .spthy files under
  examples/ were scanned; zero use `last()` in a restriction, which is what
  this bug requires.  This is a reachable trap, not evidence that published
  Tamarin results are wrong.  Scanner: tools/lastscan.py.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FIX
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PR #915.  The load-bearing line is in Guarded.hs:

      noExistential (GAto a) = not $ isLastAtom a

  `toInductionHypothesis` already refuses formulas containing `last`
  (Guarded.hs:619, "formula not last-free"), so reclassifying simply makes
  induction inapplicable to theories carrying such a restriction, which is the
  correct behaviour.
==============================================================================
*/

theory LastInductionUnsound begin

rule Start: [ Fr(x) ] --[ Start(x) ]-> [ A(x) ]
rule Step:  [ A(x)  ] --[ Step(x)  ]-> [      ]

restriction StartIsNotLast:
  "All x #i. Start(x) @ i ==> not(last(#i))"

// FALSE. Tamarin proves it because [use_induction] triggers the unsound rule.
lemma NoStart_with_induction [use_induction]:
  "All x #i. Start(x) @ i ==> F"

// The identical formula without induction. Correctly falsified.
lemma NoStart_without_induction:
  "All x #i. Start(x) @ i ==> F"

// Sanity check: the model is not degenerate, Start really is reachable.
lemma StartReachable: exists-trace
  "Ex x #i. Start(x) @ i"

end
